  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 322 AM EDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nBy Sun night The main frontal boundary across North GA finally begins \nmaking its way southward through the state. This front stalls again \nacross N FL/S GA Mon night with precip chances staying elevated \nacross central GA through Wed night. Currently seeing precip totals \nin the 2\" to 4\" range across central GA and 1 to 3 inches across \nNorth GA through Day 6. Due to the limited amount of precipitation\nwe have seen over the past few weeks, central GA can handle a bit\nmore rain than north GA but 2\" to 4.5\" is still a lot of water in\njust 5 days. Will still need to keep a close eye on the flooding \npotential across the area. Another marginal risk is out for Mon \nas well for excessive rain across central and south GA. Still not \nseeing much in the way of instability across the area but we could\nstill see some thunderstorms. SPC only has us under a general \nrisk of severe through Sun which continues to look good. \n\nBy daybreak Tue the frontal boundary will be south of the area \nwith a cooler/drier airmass filtering into the state from the \nnorth. Day time highs will be mainly in the 70s across the area \nMon through Thu.",
